# foundry-audit allowlist — one entry per line, `#` comments allowed.
#
# Two kinds of entry are recognised: a repo-relative path that is referenced
# deliberately before it exists, and an identifier (e.g. ADR-NNN) that appears
# in the documents as an example rather than as a reference.
#
# This file is not a place to silence findings you'd rather not fix. If an
# entry here is hiding something real, the check is right and the document is
# wrong. Every entry should be able to say why it's here.

# Foundry's docs describe foundry-audit's own dangling-reference check, which
# means writing out an example dangling ID. Foundry itself uses date-headed
# decisions, not ADR numbering, so this is subject matter, not a reference.
ADR-099

# SESSIONS.md (2026-08-17) recounts a real dangling reference this tool caught:
# a decision cited as 2026-06-30 when the entry is dated 2026-06-28. Telling
# that story requires writing the wrong date down next to the words
# "DECISIONS.md", which is exactly what the check looks for. Allowlisted rather
# than reworded, because the account is worth more than the silence — and there
# is no decision entry on this date to shadow. If one is ever written, delete
# this line first.
2026-06-30

# The first real archive pass (2026-10-19) moved four entries into
# DECISIONS_ARCHIVE.md, and the dangling-reference check has no archive-aware
# tier: it only looks for a '## <date>' heading in DECISIONS.md, so a reference
# to an archived entry reads as dangling even though it resolves. Every
# genuine cross-reference was repointed at the archive by name instead of being
# allowlisted (see README.md's two roadmap lines). These two dates could not be,
# and are allowlisted with the reason stated:
#
# 2026-08-11 is cited in prose inside two *surviving* decision entries
# (DECISIONS.md's 2026-08-18 conditional-offers entry). Rewording a surviving
# entry to clear a check is worse than the silence, and would not clear it
# anyway: the check prefixes every line with its filename, so every line of
# DECISIONS.md matches its 'mentions DECISIONS.md' filter and every ISO date in
# the file counts as a reference. Delete this line if that entry stops citing it.
2026-08-11

# 2026-08-17 survives only as a ship-date stamp on two README.md roadmap lines
# ('added (2026-08-17)', 'done (2026-08-17)') that happen to also say
# 'templates/DECISIONS.md.template' and to list DECISIONS.md among files a past
# pass touched. Neither is a reference to a decision; both are facts about when
# something shipped. Delete this line if a real citation of that date appears.
2026-08-17
